The framework corpus: seven real applications, built here, committed as built.

WHAT QUESTION IT WAS BUILT TO ANSWER
Whether modern frameworks are the reason real pages do not render on this
machine -- and specifically whether the framework is the variable at all, or
whether the bundler runtime under it is. React, Vue, Angular and Svelte ship
through three or four bundlers, so if the bundler is the variable the failures
collapse onto a couple of mechanisms and the ranked table is short.

THE ANSWER, measured 2026-08-08, `make probe-frameworks`:

  CAUSE                          FRAMEWORKS  CHROME         WHICH
  document.currentScript         2 of 7      ours           next, webpack
  HTMLTemplateElement.content    1 of 7      ours           svelte
  SVGElement                     1 of 7      ours           vue
  document.baseURI               1 of 7      ours           angular
  (no uncaught exception)        2 of 7      -              react, vite

THE HYPOTHESIS DID NOT HOLD, and that is the finding. The bundler runtime is
one cause of five and accounts for 2 of the 7 builds. The other three die on
three unrelated DOM primitives, in three different frameworks, all four of
which happen to route through Rollup/Vite -- the same bundler that produced the
two builds with no exceptions at all. A bundler that fails four ways and works
twice is not the variable. Every cause here is a MISSING DOM/document
PROPERTY, and the bundler only determines which one a given build reaches
first.

The corollary matters more than the refutation: there is no single fix. This is
a list of four, and it is a short list precisely because each item is small.

THE CHROME COLUMN
`tests/chrome/webapi_chromediff.mjs` (the JS-exception line's harness, used
as-is) served these exact bytes to real headless Chrome over each fixture's own
URL. Chrome threw ZERO exceptions on all seven. So nothing here is the app's own
bug -- which is the thing this corpus buys over tests/fixtures/webapi, where a
failure is entangled with the site's own code. The differential output is
committed as CHROMEDIFF.

  Caveat, stated because it is load-bearing: webapi_chromediff.mjs extracts an
  error type with /^([A-Z]\w*Error|DOMException)\b/, which cannot match the base
  class `Error`, and then discards anything with neither a type nor an
  identifier. Every bundler-runtime throw is a bare `new Error(...)`, so
  webpack's row reads NOT MEASURED rather than `ours`. It was established
  another way: Chrome fetched all three of webpack's files including the lazy
  chunk, so its chunk loader ran. Reported to that line; a one-token fix.

WHAT IS IN EACH FIXTURE
  <name>/index.html    the document the toolchain emitted, unmodified
  <name>/s###.js       every emitted script, flat
  <name>/manifest.txt  "<src as the page names it>\t<local file>", keyed twice
  <name>/SOURCE        the document's absolute URL, first line
Same layout as tests/fixtures/webapi so the SAME probe measures both and the
numbers are comparable. A fixture directory without manifest.txt is not a
fixture: the probe keys off it and silently falls back to inline scripts only.

  _src/       the app sources, so the corpus is reproducible (build_apps.sh)
  _platform/  the seven bundles' throw sites reduced to one script
  _paint/     the built documents plus one module that reads the DOM back
  BASELINE    what `make test-frameworks` asserts
  CHROMEDIFF  the differential this README's Chrome column comes from

Each app is minimal but real: a component with state, a click handler, and a
lazily imported route. The lazy route renders AT STARTUP rather than behind the
click -- the probe injects no input, so a chunk loader reachable only by a click
would never run. A hand-written <script> pulling a framework off a CDN would
exercise no bundler runtime at all and the corpus would measure nothing.

WHAT WAS PRUNED, AND WHAT THAT COSTS
Only .js/.mjs are packed; CSS, fonts, images and source maps are dropped
(pack.py). The probe fetches no sub-resource, so they cannot change a
JavaScript number, and Chrome's 404 for them lands in the network bucket the
differential already excludes on both sides. It is 1.2 MB instead of ~10 MB.
The cost is real and is not hidden: THESE FIXTURES CANNOT ANSWER "does it look
right". They answer "does it run" and "did anything reach the DOM".

DID ANYTHING RENDER -- because scripts running clean is not a page
`_paint/` reads the DOM back after the application's own module has run. Two of
the seven genuinely mount:

  react     #root 0 -> 155 chars, button reads "count is 0", lazy route rendered
  vite      #app rendered, button reads "count is 0", lazy chunk resolved
  next      #app=207 -- but next/index.html ALREADY CONTAINS that markup and the
            string "count is ": this is the static export's prerendered HTML,
            not evidence of hydration. The invariant throw is in the chunk
            registration, so nothing is wired up and the button is dead.
  angular   <app-root> is empty in the document and holds 87 chars after load,
            so Angular did bootstrap and render its template -- but the button's
            text is empty and the router outlet never fills: the lazy route dies
            in new URL(path, document.baseURI).
  svelte    #app = 0 chars. Nothing.
  vue       #app = 0 chars. Nothing.
  webpack   #app = 0 chars. Nothing.

No screenshot: painting one of these on the machine needs the ISO, the disk
image and a QMP harness serving the fixture (the shape of tests/qmp/qmp_bing.py),
and HEAD does not currently build from a clean clone. The DOM read-back is the
strongest evidence available without that, and it is stated as such rather than
as a screenshot.

OUT OF SCOPE, recorded so it does not look like an omission
Node, Express and NestJS are server-side and never run in a browser. React
Native renders to native views, and Electron/Tauri ship their own engine. None
of them is a browser target, so none is in this corpus.

REFRESHING IT
  bash tests/fixtures/frameworks/build_apps.sh    scaffold + build + pack
  make probe-frameworks-chrome                    re-take the differential
Both change the numbers, so BASELINE and this README must be re-taken with them
in the same commit.
